{align:justify|Professor Quan and his wife are now 55 years old. They have three daughters of remarkable beauty, quite far apart in age: 25, 23, and 20. Though these young ladies are as lovely as jade and gold, not one of them has ever had a suitor. The professors are beside themselves with grief. Many young men fall head over heels the moment they set eyes on the girls and pursue them ardently — but as soon as they discover that these peerless beauties cannot utter a word, they immediately retreat and walk away.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan and his wife belong to the educated upper class and never believed in the law of cause and effect. Yet having given birth one after another to three mute daughters, their spirits were battered almost to collapse. They were stunned and bewildered, filled with immeasurable suffering. Sorrow aged their faces; though highly learned and wealthy, they could bring no happiness to their own children. The affliction of the three beauties filled onlookers with awe, concern, and unanswerable questions.}
{align:justify|There is a saying: "In youth people love to discuss learning; in middle age they love to discuss destiny; in old age they love to discuss religion." Professor Quan and his wife are now entering old age, and their enduring anguish is a sickness no medicine can cure. So gradually they began to develop an interest in religion.}
{align:justify|One weekend, at a friend's encouragement, they went to a suburban temple to listen to an eminent monk expound the Dharma.}
{align:justify|The Venerable Master taught: "Good deeds bring good rewards, evil deeds bring evil retribution." The law of cause and effect exists with complete objectivity — it was not decreed or invented by the Buddha. The Buddha merely proclaimed it, explaining it clearly so that sentient beings would be warned and vigilant. Whether you believe it or not, all of our lives unfold within cause and effect, so each of us must take responsibility for our own good and evil actions. Every person is the creator of his own destiny. If you do evil yourself, you will meet with miserable retribution...}
{align:justify|The theme of that day's Dharma talk seemed to rise up precisely for Mr. Quan to contemplate and to confront the depths of his own heart... Suddenly he recalled something from 25 years before...}
{align:justify|"Back then, Professor Quan and his wife had been married only two months when Mrs. Quan became pregnant. Mr. Quan was overjoyed and drove his wife in an expensive luxury car to a scenic coastal region for a holiday.}
{align:justify|On one occasion they visited northern Thailand to attend a banquet, parking the car in the wide courtyard of a temple. The moment Mr. Quan opened the car door, a crowd of ragged street children rushed over, jostling and tugging at their sleeves, begging for money.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan despised beggars. Not only did he give nothing himself, he announced to everyone around:}
{align:justify|"Don't give them money. That only trains them in bad habits — refusing to earn an honest living, idling about on charity, and becoming a blight on society..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, the vagrant children drifted away. After the banquet, when the Quans returned to their parking spot, they discovered that their beloved brand-new luxury car had been slashed with a deep scratch running from front to back. Professor Quan erupted; blazing with fury, he assumed it must have been those street children. Scanning the surroundings, he spotted four or five children aged about 13 or 14 gathered under a nearby mango tree. He charged over and, without asking who was right or wrong, slapped every one of them hard across the face. Then he loudly demanded they confess who had scratched his precious car. When no one would own up, he threatened to haul them to the police station. Terrified, the oldest of them pointed into the distance — at a ragged little beggar boy who was begging from some foreigners — and said he was the culprit. Still raging, Professor Quan stormed over, seized the boy, and dragged him to the car, while the other children seized the chance to scatter and escape.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan savagely slapped both cheeks of the child and fiercely interrogated him: why had he dared to scratch the car? Only after questioning him for some time did he discover something: the boy was mute. The child kept gesturing helplessly with his hands, tears brimming in his terrified eyes, his gaze utterly pitiful. Professor Quan could not understand sign language and concluded the boy must have borne a grudge over his "give them no money" remark and vandalized the car out of spite. So in fury he lashed out with his right foot, kicking the mute boy hard in the chest. With a dull thud the boy toppled over, legs in the air — and then, with a retching sound, bright red blood gushed from his mouth. The bystanders rushed in, pulled the professor away, and carried the mute child off so he would be beaten no more.}
{align:justify|As the mute boy was led away, he kept turning back to glare at the professor, his eyes full of hatred. Afterward the mute child took shelter in the temple; he fell gravely ill and within a few years became disabled. Everyone knew the mute boy was good-natured at heart — he had never scratched the car. Professor Quan had beaten an innocent child.}
{align:justify|At the end of that year, Mrs. Quan gave birth to a baby girl with delicate brows and bright eyes — but by age three she still could not speak. Then Mrs. Quan bore a second daughter, who likewise could not speak by age three. Finally she bore a third daughter, and the moment she was born they hurriedly called a doctor to examine her: yet another mute little girl! Fearing that a fourth or fifth child would also be a mute daughter, Mrs. Quan asked the doctor to sterilize her."}
{align:justify|Now, every time Professor Quan looks at his three mute daughters, he recalls that merciless kick he dealt the mute boy long ago. He cannot forget the sight of bright blood spurting from the boy's mouth, nor that terrifying, hate-filled stare...}
{align:justify|Even today the memory makes him shudder. He has come to understand and can no longer disbelieve the principle of karmic retribution — the law of Heaven that turns in perfect fairness, a law of nature itself. For that brutal act of 25 years ago, regret now comes far too late.}
{align:justify|Since then, every weekend Professor Quan and his wife go to the temple to hear the eminent monks expound the sutras, repenting their sins before the Buddha. They have vowed to cultivate goodness and accumulate virtue. As the proverb says: "Only at fifty does a man finally see the faults of his forty-ninth year."}
{align:justify|Commentary:}
{align:justify|Having translated this story, I am reminded of a tale of cause and effect from the Tang dynasty:}
{align:justify|There was a farmer of extremely cruel nature. One morning he went out to inspect his fields and saw his neighbor's water buffalo eating his rice shoots and trampling his paddies into a mess. Exploding with rage, he seized the buffalo by the head, drew a sharp knife, and cut out the animal's tongue.}
{align:justify|Ten years later the farmer married and had three children — but all of them were mute. He sought famous physicians everywhere to cure his children, all in vain. He could not understand why his children were like this, and his heart ached beyond measure. One night he dreamed of the scene in which he had cruelly cut out the buffalo's tongue. Waking, he understood everything: because of that evil deed, his children now suffered this calamity. In truth, it was only right that the farmer himself should bear the bitter retribution — but why were his children also afflicted? This can only be explained in Buddhist terms as collective karma: those who commit similar evil together will receive the same retribution together.}
